{
  "verdict": "divergent",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "The conclusion drops the negation inside the second occurrence of K. Theorem 3 requires `𝓢 ⊢ K ψ ⋏ K (∼(F ψ))`, but the TARGET says `𝓢 ⊢ K ψ ⋏ K (F ψ)`. It asserts knowledge that F holds of the witness rather than knowledge that F does not hold of it."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "In the declared abstraction, `𝓑 ⪯ 𝓢` expresses inclusion of provable sentences, but `𝓑` is not required to be EA. Arithmetic, object-language quantifiers, and the full EA axioms for K are not represented; base necessitation and the distribution of K over modus ponens in `𝓢` are supplied explicitly."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The source obtains every `F(⌜φ⌝)` by instantiating one fixed one-free-variable formula with a sentence code. Lean instead uses a sentence function `F` with no formula-definability requirement and assumes its negative fixed point via `Def5`, rather than obtaining that fixed point by diagonalization."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The pointwise hypothesis correctly places the whole implication `F φ 🡒 φ` under K. Even allowing for the declared abstraction, the missing negation changes what the theorem establishes about its witness."
}
